<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>
<!-- Generated by Pandoc using pretext.lua -->

<exercises xml:id="ex-6-5-Linear_vs_exp_point-slope" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
	<title component="not-workbook">Exercises</title>

	<xi:include href="ex-intro.ptx" />
    
    <exercise xml:id="parents-house-lve-point-slope">
        <introduction>
            <p>
                My parents bought the house I grew up in for $35,000 in 1964 and sold it 40 years later (in 2004) for $342,000. True story.  (It was before the housing bubble burst.)
                <aside>
                    <p>
                        Story also appears in 
                        <xref ref="ex-5-4-Linear_vs_exponential" text="custom">
                            5.4 Exercises
                        </xref>.
                    </p>
                </aside>
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Write a linear and exponential model and compare the house's value in 2026 according to each model. Don't forget to name the variables, including units. Use the actual year.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The house is valued at $687,000 in 2026.
                    <aside>
                        <p>
                            Source: Zillow
                        </p>
                    </aside> 
                    How well did your linear and exponential models predict this current value?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="manufacturing-jobs-lve-point-slope">
        <introduction>
            <p>
                The number of manufacturing jobs in the state has been declining for decades. In 1970, there were 1.2 million such jobs in the state but by 2010 there were only 0.6 million such jobs. 
                <aside>
                    <p>
                        Story also appears in 
                        <xref ref="ex-5-4-Linear_vs_exponential" text="custom">
                            5.4 Exercises
                        </xref>.
                    </p>
                </aside>
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Write a linear and exponential model and compare the predicted number of manufacturing jobs in 2025 according to each model. Use the actual year.  
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Compare to the actual number, which is reported to be 12.5 million in 2025.
                    <aside>
                        <p>
                            Source: <url href="https://www.macrotrends.net/3109/us-manufacturing-employment">Macrotrends</url>
                        </p>
                    </aside>
                    What can you say about manufacturing jobs in the late 1900s/early 2000s versus now?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="asthma-lve-point-slope">
        <introduction>
            <p>
                The number of asthma sufferers worldwide in 1990 was 84 million
                and 130 million in 2001.
                <aside>
                    <p>
                        Story also appears in 
                        <xref ref="ex-5-4-Linear_vs_exponential" text="custom">
                            5.4 Exercises
                        </xref>.
                    </p>
                </aside>
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Compare what the linear and exponential models project for
                    the year 2023 and 2030. Use the actual year.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Compare to the actual number which was reported to be 363
                    million in 2023.
                    <aside><p>Source: <url href="https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/asthma">WHO</url></p></aside>
                    Which model is closer?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="hybrid-cars-lve-point-slope">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Sales of hybrid cars in the United States have continued to
                increase. In 1999, 17 (yes, seventeen) hybrid cars were sold. By
                2002, that number was up to 34,521 hybrid cars sold. Compare
                what the linear and exponential models projected for hybrid car
                sales in the year 2025 and compare to the actual number which is
                reported to be 2.05 million in 2025, which doesn't even include
                electric cars.
                <aside><p>Source: <url href="https://www.nada.org/nada/nada-headlines/december-2025-market-beat-new-light-vehicle-sales-totaled-162-million-units">nada.org</url></p></aside>
                Use the actual year.
                <aside>
                    <p>
                        Story also appears in 
                        <xref ref="ex-5-4-Linear_vs_exponential" text="custom">
                            5.4 Exercises
                        </xref>.
                    </p>
                </aside>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>
</exercises>